{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Fresh field evidence (2026-08-20) — candidate driver of machine-wide saturation\n\nToday's session ran up to 5 concurrent agents, each repeatedly resolving attested gates\n(`review-plan`, `verify-completion`, `review-code`). Observed on the host:\n\n- sustained load average 12.02 (15-min 8.53, i.e. still climbing), 19 rebar-related processes\n- at one point three processes pinned at ~95% CPU simultaneously, from a worktree unrelated\n  to the work I was driving\n- two subagents stalled outright, twice in a row, and the harness's own tooling began timing\n  out — symptoms of resource starvation rather than agent fault\n\nIf every attested gate resolution materialises ~620 MiB / 69,984 blobs with no reuse, as this\nticket measures, then N concurrent agents running gates multiply that directly. Five agents\ncycling gates is single-digit GiB of fresh file writes per round, on a key that changes every\n~26 seconds so it effectively never hits.\n\n**Stated honestly: this is a plausible contributor, not a proven cause.** I did not instrument\nthe snapshot store during the saturation window, and I first mis-attributed the load to another\nsession's test suite. What would prove it: watch `<store>/tickets-<sha>` entry creation and\nbytes written while a gate runs, and correlate with the CPU spikes.\n\nWhy it matters for prioritisation: this ticket is currently framed as a write-amplification and\ndisk-waste problem. The multi-agent interaction makes it a **throughput** problem as well — the\ncost lands on whichever machine is running the agents, and it scales with the concurrency this\nproject actively encourages. That argues for raising it above P2, or at least for recording the\nconcurrency multiplier in the ticket's framing.\n\nRelated and worth sequencing together: `moist-short-lionfish` (linear drain-gate walk on every\nwrite) hits the same store from the write side, and `carryable-basalt-beagle` (leak-guard\nfixture deleting concurrent writes under REPO_ROOT) is a third instance of the same theme —\nconcurrent sessions sharing one repo root with no coordination.\n\nNo description edit — evidence in comments to keep any attestation current."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787250755051044001,"uuid":"fcef0ded-2e24-4ded-b124-6a5588858eb6"}